Oyo Nyimba, once recognized as the world's youngest reigning monarch, has died in his mid-30s. As one of Uganda's traditional monarchs, he took the throne of the Tooro kingdom in 1995, aged just 3.

Oyo Nyimba, a Ugandan king of the traditional pre-colonial kingdom of Tooro, has died aged just 34, the prime minister of Tooro announced late on Thursday.&#160;

"It is with profound sadness and a heavy heart that I announce the passing of His Majesty Omukama of Tooro, King Oyo Nyimba Kabamba Iguru Rukidi IV," Tooro's regional leader Calvin Armstrong Rwomiire Akiiki said on social media.&#160;

Uganda is a republic, ruled by a parliament and a nominally-elected head of state, longstanding President Yoweri Museveni , who's been in power since 1986.&#160;

But the country retains several traditional monarchies that still preside over their kingdoms as they were constituted in the pre-colonial period. Their power is largely symbolic but they wield considerable cultural and social influence.

Although they were banned after independence from British colonial rule in 1962 by a republican president who said he wanted to unify the nascent country, Museveni reinstated the traditional monarchs in 1993.&#160;

Oyo Nyimba was arguably the most recognizable among them, not least because of his unusual story, taking the throne at the age of just 3 in 1995.&#160;

Regional leader Akiiki said that the king had died late on Thursday, without giving a cause of death. He said an official statement with further details, including the funeral and mourning arrangements, would follow later.&#160;

He called on the people of Tooro to "remain united, clam and prayerful during this difficult time."&#160;

Akiiki also issued one surprising claim about the unmarried young king, who since a very young age had been seen as perhaps Uganda's most eligible bachelor.&#160;

"The continuity of the Crown is assured," he said. "His Majesty leaves behind a Prince as heir, whose identity will be formally revealed at the appropriate time, in accordance with the traditions and customs of the Torro kingdom."&#160;

Oyo Nyimba's death triggered an outpouring of grief online and widespread national coverage in Uganda.&#160;

Born on April 16, 1992, Oyo was the second-born child of the late Omukama Patrick David Matthew Kavoyo Olimi III and Queen Best Kemigisa Kaboyo, with two sisters.

He ascended to the throne of Tooro, an area in western Uganda near the border to Congo in 1995 after his father's death.&#160;

His unusual status as a modern "boy king" led to considerable interest early in his reign, including from world leaders like former Libyan strongman Moammar Gadhafi, who repeatedly visited Tooro.

